Fossil fuel combustion from stationary sources makes up the majority of the total anthropogenic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ contributions, raising huge environmental challenges facing our planet. [1] Although aqueous alkanolamine solutions are the state-ofthe-art capture absorbents that broadly implemented in power plants for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture, the regeneration of them from carbamates inevitably leads to a huge energy penalty.[2] Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), constructed from organic links and metal ions or clusters, represent one of the most promising materials for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture and storage.[2c,3] Owing to their high porosity, structural diversity, tunable pore environment, and atomically well-defined skeleton, MOFs have been extensively explored in various applications, such as gas storage and separation, catalysis, and chemo-sensing.[4]
Increasing MOF surface area and pore size is one of the most efficient approaches to maximize $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake and can dramatically enhance the storage capacity under high pressure $\left(>3{\cdot}5\ \mathrm{MPa}\right)$ .[5] But such high adsorption is often not viable when used in a post-combustion power plant where the pressure of the flue gas from smoke stacks usually maintains below $0.3~\mathrm{MPa}$ .[6] Indeed, pore surface functionalization of MOFs is the most widely used method for the efficient and selective carbon capture. Generally, functional groups with high polarity, such as pyridine, -OH, $\mathrm{-NO}_{2},$ , -CN, -SH, etc., and/or open metal sites decorating the walls of MOFs pores are favorable, since $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecule processes a large quadrupole moment and these functional sites are able to induce polarization and enhance affinity. [3a,3b,7] Another effective way to improve the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capacity and selectivity is to anchor basic amine groups onto the skeleton of MOFs to mimic the chemisorption in the liquid absorbents.[8] Nevertheless, aside from ${\sim}75\%\ \mathrm{N}_{2}$ and ${\sim}15\%\mathrm{CO}_{2},$ a typical postcombustion flue gas also contains $5\%\sim7\%$ water, which must be thoroughly took into consideration for real applications. $[2\mathrm{c},3\mathrm{a},3\mathrm{b},9]$ During the separation process, water molecules with higher polarity and binding energy will strongly compete against $\mathrm{CO}_{2},$ and therefore, the active adsorptive sites in MOFs are easily poisoned by only small amount of water. Consequently, the capacity and selectivity will be dramatically dampened under real humid flue gas conditions.[10]
Needless to say that many MOF structures are vulnerable under moisture conditions, and the collapse of the framework by slow hydrolysis can significantly lower the separation performance and impede their practical application.[10c,11] Alternatively, trapping $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in a confined space offers opportunities to separate gas molecules based on size. Due to the difficulties in the design and synthesis of MOFs with pore openings that exactly match for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecule in kinetic diameter, only few pioneering works have been reported.[12] Therefore, the demand of a facile method to prepare moisture-stable MOF materials that can selectively adsorb $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ over other gas components and water molecules from flue gas is ever urgent yet largely unmet.
## Journal of the American Chemical Society
Recently, Cohen et al. reported polymer−metal−organic frameworks that can selective adsorption of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ against $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ and exhibited high water stability.[13] Kitagawa et al. are pioneered in in-situ polymerization of vinyl monomers inside the channels of MOFs.[14] Inspired by their works, herein, we report a strategy that can divide the open channels of MOFs into confined and hydrophobic compartments by in-situ polymerization of aromatic acetylenes inside MOF pores. Thus-obtained MOF material can capture and trap $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules and effectively retard the diffusion and repel water molecules. We intentionally selected MOF-5, a famous and highly porous MOF structure that can also be produced in industrial scale $\cdot>1$ ton per 30 mins)[15], to serve as the prototype host material. 1,2-diethynylbenzene (DEB) as monomer were adsorbed and encapsulated in MOF-5 and further heated at elevated temperature to afford polynaphthylene (PN) inside the channels via Bergman cyclization and subsequent radical polymerization (Scheme 1), and this composite is denoted as PN@MOF-5. Due to its hydrophobic and linear nature, PNs in MOF-5 can act as partitions to segregate the micropores with ${\sim}1.2\ \mathrm{nm}$ in width into ultra micropores $\left(\leq0.7{\mathrm{nm}}\right)$ and simultaneously improve the stability of $\mathrm{PN}@\mathrm{MOF}{\cdot}S$ towards moisture.
In contrast to MOF-5, which is water-sensitive, $\mathrm{PN}@\mathrm{MOF}{\cdot}S$ can retain its crystallinity and porosity upon exposure to humid air $\left(>40\mathrm{h,RH}=40\%\right)$ . More importantly, the ultra micropores and exposed large amount of aromatic edges and surfaces in $\mathrm{PN}@\mathrm{MOF}.5$ allow its efficient capture of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ (Scheme 1). Compared with pristine MOF-5, the thermodynamic adsorption capacity of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ is doubled and selectivity of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm N_{2}$ (14/86, 1 bar, 273 K) is 23-times increased for $\mathrm{PN}@\mathrm{MOF}{\cdot}S$ . Furthermore, in breakthrough experiments, $\mathrm{PN}@\mathrm{MOF}{\cdot}5$ largely maintained its dynamic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capacity under humid conditions $\textgreater90\%$ retention rate under $\mathrm{RH}=65\%$ ).
The details for the polymerization of DEB in MOF-5 were described in Supporting Information. A significant color change from white to brown was observed after heating (Figures S3 and S4), indicative of the formation of highly conjugated polymers. The powder X-ray diffraction pattern (PXRD) of $\mathrm{PN}@\mathrm{MOF}.5$ is consistent with that of MOF-5 (Figure S5), revealing the crystalline structure is retained during inclusion and polymerization of DEB. $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ solid state NMR spectra of $\mathrm{PN}@\mathrm{MOF}.5$ as well as the PN polymer isolated from $\mathrm{PN}@\mathrm{MOF}.5$ (Figure S7) display the signals of aromatic carbons belonging to the polynaphthylene at ${\sim}128$ ppm. The disappearance of $C{\equiv}C{\cdot}\mathrm{H}$ stretching vibration at $3300~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ and $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C}}\equiv{\mathrm{C}}-{\mathrm{H}}$ bending vibration at ${\sim}700~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ in the fourier transform infrared attenuated total reflection (FTIRATR) spectrum of $\mathrm{PN}@\mathrm{MOF}.5$ (Figure S8) reflects the high polymerization degree of acetylene. As determined by elemental analysis, the loading amount of PN in the hostpolymer inclusion is $15.0~\mathrm{wt\%}$ (Table S1). The PN loading can be adjusted by carefully altering the amount of solvent used for washing before polymerization, and accordingly, another three inclusion samples with a 3.2, 34 and $40\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ PN loading (denoted as $\mathrm{PN}_{\mathrm{x}}@\mathrm{MOF}{-}5$ , x means loading amount%) were obtained. Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) and optical microscopy images (Figures S9 and S10) of the two $\mathrm{PN}@\mathrm{MOF}{\cdot}S$ samples display their preserved crystal morphologies. To address whether the PN polymers are homogeneously existed in large domains (greater than a nanometer scale), each crystal was dissected into two segments. The color is almost evenly distributed in each crystal, and the higher the loading amount, the deeper the color.
Nitrogen sorption isotherm tests at $77~\mathrm{K}$ were conducted to access their porosities. Analyses of the sorption curves of MOF-5, $\mathrm{PN}_{3.2}@\mathrm{MOF}{-5}$ and $\mathrm{PN}@\mathrm{MOF}.5$ (Figure S11) by the Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) method give specific surface areas of 3200, 2600 and $1200\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively (Figures S12-S14). Quenched solid-state density functional theory (QSDFT) was utilized to deduce the pore size distributions (Figure 1a). Interestingly, along with the increasing of PN loadings, the pores with $1.2\ \mathrm{nm}$ (MOF-5 pores) in
