width are gradually diminished whereas the pores with 0.6 nm (partitioned pores) in width are emerged and boosted significantly. These results demonstrate that the PN polymers can act as partitions to segregate the channels of the crystals into confined compartments. Although these analyses do not preclude the presence of PN polymers wrapped the surface of crystals, we believe that the overall consideration of the pore size distribution analysis, PXRD measurements and microscopy images strongly support that most PN polymers are distributed inside the crystal channels and serve as partitions.
Compartments with confined space together with abundant exposed surfaces and edges of aromatic rings in PN@MOF-5 are supposed to be favorable for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture.[16] Considering a balance between partitioning the MOF channels and partially blocking (Figure S15 and S16), we selected $\mathrm{PN}@\mathrm{MOF}{\cdot}5$ to perform $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption isotherm test at different temperatures and calculated its adsorption enthalpy. Remarkably, compared with MOF-5 $38\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at 273 K, 760 Torr), the doubled $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capacity of $\mathrm{PN}@\mathrm{MOF}.5$ (Figure 1b) with just one third of the BET surface area of MOF-5 is achieved $\left(78\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\right.$ at 273 K, 760 Torr). It is worth noted that, derived from $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption isotherm at $273\mathrm{~K~}$ by using NLDFT method, the pore size distribution (Figure S17) displays a maximum at about $0.5\substack{-0.6\mathrm{nm}}$ as well as partial pores at 0.37 and $0.81~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . We further employed the Clausius-Clapeyron formula to determine the isosteric heats of adsorption $(\mathrm{Q}_{s t})$ from $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms at 273, 283 and $298{\mathrm{~K~}}$ (Figures 1b and S18). At zero loading the $\mathrm{Q}_{\mathrm{st}}$ value $\left(-\Delta H\right)$ is $29\mathrm{\kJ/mol},$ , and at higher loadings it decreases to ${\sim}24\ \mathrm{kJ/mol}$ , the value of which is still one third higher than that of MOF-5. The fact that twice amount of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ are adsorbed in the comparatively low-surface-area material of $\mathrm{PN}@\mathrm{MOF}{\cdot}5$ with larger $\mathrm{Q}_{\mathrm{st}}$ can be attributed to the enhanced adsorbate-surface interactions to both sides or ends of the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules stemmed from confined pore size effect and the more exposed aromatic surfaces.
Ideal adsorption solution theory (IAST) was applied in order to predict the expected selectivity of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and ${\bf N}_{2}$ for the materials (Figure 3a). Under simulated post-combustion flue gas composition $\left(14\%\mathrm{CO}_{2},86\%\mathrm{N}_{2}\right)$ , the calculated $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm N_{2}$ selectivity at 1 bar and $273\mathrm{K}$ for PN@MOF-5 (212) is almost 23 times higher than that of MOF-5 (9) (Table S2).
MOF-5 is very sensitive to moisture, as a result of the breakdown of the coordination bonds by the attack of water molecule.[17] In contrast to the hydrophilic nature of MOF-5 with a water contact angle close to $0^{\circ}$ , $\mathrm{PN}@\mathrm{MOF}{\cdot}5$ is hydrophobic and exhibits a water contact angle of $135^{\circ}$ (Figure 2a). MOF-5 completely loses its porosities within $6\mathrm{~h~}$ and transforms to MOF- $69\mathsf{c}^{[18]}$ within $40\mathrm{~h~}$ in a humid environment $\mathrm{'RH}=40\%$ ), as evidenced by PXRD and ${\bf N}_{2}$ sorption isotherm measurements (Figures 2b and S23). On the contrary, the BET surface area and crystallinity of $\mathrm{PN}@\mathrm{MOF}.5$ are preserved after $40\mathrm{-h}$ moisture treatment (Figures 2b and S23), indicating that the inclusion of aromatic PN into the channels can effectively prevent the attack to the coordination bonds from water. Similar results are observed for $\mathrm{PN}_{3.2}@\mathrm{MOF}{-5}$ (Figures S24 and S25).
Given the high humid stability, hydrophobicity and good $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake capacity, we carried out dynamic separation experiments to test the ability of $\mathrm{PN}@\mathrm{MOF}.5$ to separate $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ from ${\bf N}_{2}$ with and without moisture (see details in Figure S26 in the SI). A mixed $\Nu_{2}/\Nu\phantom{+}$ gas with $16\%\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ content was introduced to the bed, and the effluent was monitored by a mass spectrometer (Figure 3b and 3c). Under dry conditions, $\mathrm{PN}@\mathrm{MOF}.5$ adsorbent bed shows a dynamic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacity of $34~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g},$ which is one-and-ahalf times of MOF-5 $\left(23\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}\right)$ prepared under similar conditions. For humid condition test, a gas mixture $\left(\mathrm{V}\big(\mathrm{N}_{2}\big)/\mathrm{V}\big(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\big)=84{:}16\right)$ with a RH value of $65\%$ was introduced to the bed. The dynamic sorption capacity of $\mathrm{PN}@\mathrm{MOF}.5$ under moisture almost retains the value obtained under dry condition, while MOF-5 displays a $40\%$ and $73\%$ decrease in the first and second cycle (Figures 3b and 3c), respectively, in the presence of water. In addition, the topology of $\mathrm{PN}@\mathrm{MOF}.5$ remained intact after dynamic sorption, while the skeleton of MOF-5 was almost collapsed (Figure S29). For comparison, two representative MOFs, MOF-199 and $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66 significantly lost their $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capacity under humid conditions (Table S3).
In conclusion, we have reported a new strategy to divide MOF pores into confined compartments by in-situ polymerization of aromatic acetylene. For PN@MOF-5, a remarkable increase in $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacity from 38 to $78\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm N_{2}$ selectivity from 9 to 212 at $273\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1 bar were obtained. Due to its hydrophobic nature, not only the stability of the framework toward moisture was significantly improved but also the competitive adsorption of water against $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecule was effectively inhibited. Consequently, the dynamic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capacity under humid conditions reached a $94\%$ retention rate. The improved stability towards moisture and increased adsorption capacity of PN@UMCM-8 (Figure S32-S34) demonstrates the applicability of the present strategy. The abundant combinations of porous materials (i.e., MOFs, covalent organic frameworks,[19] porous polymers, etc.) and conventional polymers will provide versatile material platforms for achieving multi-functions and practical applications.
